  "name": "RBC FOLATE",
  "code": "1191",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul class=\"marker:text-textOff list-disc\">\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Normal:</strong>&nbsp;RBC folate level within reference range indicates adequate folate stores in red blood cells.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Low RBC Folate:</strong>&nbsp;Suggestive of folate deficiency which may cause megaloblastic anemia, fatigue, irritability, and increased risk for neural tube defects in pregnancy. Possible causes include poor dietary intake, malabsorption syndromes (e.g., celiac disease, Crohn&rsquo;s disease), or certain medications interfering with folate metabolism.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>High RBC Folate:</strong>&nbsp;May occur with supplementation or certain hematologic conditions. High folate may mask vitamin B12 deficiency; concurrent B12 testing is recommended.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\">\n<p class=\"my-0\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Notes:</strong> RBC folate levels reflect long-term folate status more reliably than serum folate, but both tests generally correlate closely. Variability may occur due to specimen handling, patient hematocrit, or genetic factors (e.g., MTHFR mutations).</span></p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
